WEBVTT

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:02.040
Well, shortcuts are a response to

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:03.160
the nightmare of data duplication,

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:04.920
which is a huge problem.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.899
Massive. In legacy architectures, if

00:00:07.779 --> 00:00:09.920
the machine learning team needed the

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:11.600
sales data for a forecasting model

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.399
and the finance team needed it

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.319
for a quarterly report, the data

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.340
engineers would literally copy the

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:18.880
data into two different databases.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:20.540
And then they get out of

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:21.880
sync. Within months.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:24.020
One database updates differently than

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:25.740
the other, and you completely lose

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:26.700
your single source of truth.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.160
Supported shortcuts in OneLake allow

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.060
different workspaces to reference data

00:00:30.939 --> 00:00:32.779
that physically live somewhere else,

00:00:33.019 --> 00:00:35.019
even in external cloud providers like

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:36.359
AWS or Google Cloud.
